{
  "schema": 1,
  "about": "Ground-truth corpus for EigenScript's observer predicates (#1746, #1667). Each row is a sequence with a closed form or recurrence; each checkpoint `at` = n means the observer has seen x_0..x_n. Every predicate at every checkpoint is labelled MATH (a standard definition decides it; value + citation + reason) or EIGS-DEFINED (no standard definition; the documented intent in `definitions` is ours). Verdict form: [\"MATH\", value, [source keys], reason] or [\"EIGS-DEFINED\"].",
  "params": {"rtol": 0.001, "scale": 0.001, "epsilon": "eps(L) = rtol * max(|L|, scale): the observer's declared settle tolerance (docs/SPEC.md, dh_zero and scale defaults)"},
  "sources": {
    "RUDIN_3_1": "W. Rudin, Principles of Mathematical Analysis, 3rd ed. (1976), Def. 3.1 (convergent sequence) and Thm. 3.11 (in R^k a sequence converges iff it is Cauchy).",
    "ISCLOSE": "Mixed absolute/relative tolerance |a - b| <= atol + rtol*|b|: numpy.isclose, https://numpy.org/doc/stable/reference/generated/numpy.isclose.html (the stopping criterion the observer documents in docs/PREDICATES.md).",
    "RUDIN_3_15": "W. Rudin, Principles of Mathematical Analysis, 3rd ed., Def. 3.15 (s_n -> +inf: for every real M there is N with s_n >= M for all n >= N; likewise -inf).",
    "RUDIN_3_28": "W. Rudin, Principles of Mathematical Analysis, 3rd ed., Thm. 3.28 (sum 1/n^p converges iff p > 1; the harmonic series diverges).",
    "RUDIN_3_34": "W. Rudin, Principles of Mathematical Analysis, 3rd ed., Thm. 3.34 (ratio test).",
    "RUDIN_3_43": "W. Rudin, Principles of Mathematical Analysis, 3rd ed., Thm. 3.43 (alternating series with terms decreasing to 0 converges); Leibniz bound |S - S_n| <= a_{n+1}.",
    "NW_2_2": "J. Nocedal, S. Wright, Numerical Optimization, 2nd ed. (2006), Sec. 2.2 'Rates of convergence' (Q-linear: |x_{k+1}-x*|/|x_k-x*| <= r < 1 for all k large; Q-superlinear, Q-quadratic; sublinear when the ratio tends to 1).",
    "DEVANEY_1_3": "R. L. Devaney, An Introduction to Chaotic Dynamical Systems, 2nd ed. (1989), Sec. 1.3 (periodic points, prime period, eventually periodic points) and Sec. 1.4-1.5 (attracting cycles).",
    "STROGATZ_10": "S. H. Strogatz, Nonlinear Dynamics and Chaos (1994), Ch. 10: Sec. 10.1 (fixed points of maps, multiplier f'(x*)), 10.2-10.3 (logistic map: stable fixed point for 1 < r < 3, 2-cycle for 3 < r < 3.449, 4-cycle for 3.449 < r < 3.544, chaos beyond r_inf = 3.5699 outside windows), 10.5 (Lyapunov exponent lambda = lim (1/n) sum ln|f'(x_i)|; lambda > 0 is chaos).",
    "WEYL_1916": "H. Weyl, 'Ueber die Gleichverteilung von Zahlen mod. Eins', Math. Ann. 77 (1916): n*theta mod 1 is equidistributed for irrational theta, so sin(n) is not eventually periodic and its limit set is [-1, 1].",
    "POLYA_1921": "G. Polya, 'Ueber eine Aufgabe der Wahrscheinlichkeitsrechnung betreffend die Irrfahrt im Strassennetz', Math. Ann. 84 (1921): the 1-D simple random walk is recurrent, so a.s. it neither converges nor tends to +-inf.",
    "DURRETT_BC": "R. Durrett, Probability: Theory and Examples, 5th ed. (2019), Sec. 2.3 (second Borel-Cantelli lemma: independent events with divergent probability sum occur infinitely often a.s.), so a non-degenerate iid sequence a.s. does not converge.",
    "NIST_6442": "NIST/SEMATECH e-Handbook of Statistical Methods, Sec. 6.4.4.2 'Stationarity': a stationary process has constant mean, variance and autocorrelation over time. https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmc/section4/pmc442.htm",
    "HAMILTON_15": "J. D. Hamilton, Time Series Analysis (1994), Ch. 15 (unit-root processes: the random walk y_t = y_{t-1} + e_t has Var(y_t) = t*sigma^2 and is not stationary).",
    "SHNOL_2007": "E. Shnol, 'Stability of equilibria', Scholarpedia 2(3):2770 (2007): Lyapunov stability concerns perturbations of initial conditions of an equilibrium, not one observed trajectory."
  },
  "definitions": {
    "converged": {"label": "MATH", "cite": ["RUDIN_3_1", "ISCLOSE"], "text": "Rudin Def. 3.1 instantiated at the observer's declared tolerance: converged at index n iff there is an L with |x_m - L| <= eps(L) for EVERY m >= n, eps(L) = rtol*max(|L|, scale). A tail property: it quantifies over the future, which no causal window sees (the level-shift row at n=103 and the harmonic row measure that limit)."},
    "diverging": {"label": "MATH", "cite": ["RUDIN_3_15"], "text": "Rudin Def. 3.15: x_n -> +inf or x_n -> -inf. Asymptotic, so it has one value per row. Bounded, convergent, periodic, chaotic and recurrent sequences do not diverge in this sense."},
    "oscillating": {"label": "MATH", "cite": ["DEVANEY_1_3", "STROGATZ_10"], "text": "Asymptotically periodic with prime period p >= 2: the sequence approaches a cycle of prime period >= 2 whose amplitude exceeds eps. Convergent (including damped and alternating approaches), divergent, quasi-periodic, chaotic and iid sequences are NOT oscillating in this sense. Asymptotic: transient oscillation of a damped sequence does not count."},
    "improving": {"label": "MATH", "cite": ["NW_2_2", "RUDIN_3_34"], "text": "The sequence converges to L at least Q-linearly (limsup |x_{k+1}-L|/|x_k-L| < 1; Nocedal-Wright Sec. 2.2, the ratio test) AND is not yet converged at n. Direction-agnostic: a loss rising to a plateau is improving in this sense, while the ML sense (Keras mode=min) would call it worse. Sublinear convergence (ratio -> 1, 1/n-type tails) is NOT improving."},
    "equilibrium": {"label": "MATH on some rows, EIGS-DEFINED on the rest", "cite": ["NIST_6442", "HAMILTON_15"], "text": "MATH rows: weak (level) stationarity of the tail process (constant mean, variance, autocorrelation). Labelled MATH only where it is unambiguous: constants and iid noise (true); trends, divergent sequences and the random walk (false). Elsewhere EIGS-DEFINED: for deterministic transients, cycles and chaos, stationarity needs a choice (random phase, invariant measure) no source fixes. Documented intent (docs/PREDICATES.md): balanced motion, window |mean(rel)| < dh_zero and variance(rel) < dh_zero^2."},
    "stable": {"label": "EIGS-DEFINED", "cite": ["SHNOL_2007"], "text": "No standard definition applies: Lyapunov stability needs perturbed initial conditions, not one trajectory. Documented intent (docs/PREDICATES.md): the small-motion band, every relative step under dh_small with no strong consecutive sign flips; may carry drift."}
  },
  "rows": [
